{
  "dom_id": "step-q115853",
  "name": "Question 18",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044310/assets/a355d9b35a7ebf791a56116b0ee5b127d0dfd90d6958b5bebd04aa9ad09a9ddc.png)\n\nIdentify the differential equation that generated the slope field above.",
  "worked_solution": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044310/assets/6293d40f222d4d81236808f7ab0a35dfa82e1187b3a4d24bbc841a1b158f1039.png)\n\n \n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- First, the solution curves have vertical asymptotes $x=-2$ and $x=1.$\n\nOf the given equations, the only two that satisfy this condition are\n\n$$\n{y}^{\u2032}=\\frac{1}{2-x-{x}^{2}}=-\\frac{1}{(x-1)(x+2)}\n$$\n\n \nand\n\n$$\n{y}^{\u2032}=\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+x-2}=\\frac{x}{(x-1)(x+2)}.\n$$\n\n- Second, the lines $x=-2$ and $x=1$ divide the plane into $3$ regions. The sign of the slopes in each of the $3$ regions are easily deduced by considering the behavior of the solution curves in these regions. This information is summarized in the following table:\n\n| Region | $x<-2$ | $-2<x<1$ | $x>1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Slope | $\u2198$ | $\u2197$ | $\u2198$ |\n| Sign | $-$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n\nOf the two remaining options, the only one that gives this behavior is\n\n$$\n{y}^{\u2032}=\\frac{1}{2-x-{x}^{2}}.\n$$",
